WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.076
File contents are untrusted input.

2
00:00:02.076 --> 00:00:10.380
JSON, CSV, XML, YAML, images, PDFs, office documents, and custom formats
each require parsers with different assumptions and failure modes.

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:16.192
A file may be malformed, oversized, misleading, or crafted in ways that
stress a parser or workflow.

4
00:00:16.192 --> 00:00:25.534
Safe design chooses parsers deliberately, limits resources, validates
expected structure, and handles parse failures without exposing sensitive
details.

5
00:00:25.534 --> 00:00:27.956
Deserialization deserves special care.

6
00:00:27.956 --> 00:00:38.197
Python's pickle format is powerful but should not be used with untrusted
data because loading data can influence object creation and dangerous
behavior depending on context.

7
00:00:38.197 --> 00:00:47.400
YAML and XML parser safety concepts also matter because parser defaults
and features can create risk if they process untrusted content without
constraints.

8
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:54.735
The safest parser is the one appropriate for the data, configured for the
trust boundary, and reviewed for expected behavior.

9
00:00:54.735 --> 00:01:07.744
Archive extraction should be reviewed carefully because archives can
contain many files, nested paths, unexpected names, large content,
duplicate entries, metadata, and compression behavior that affects
extraction.

10
00:01:07.744 --> 00:01:16.601
The application should decide which members are allowed, where extraction
may occur, how much data may be expanded, and what happens to rejected
files.

11
00:01:16.601 --> 00:01:21.306
Extraction is not just reading one file; it is creating many filesystem
effects.

12
00:01:21.306 --> 00:01:24.143
Metadata can carry security and privacy impact.

13
00:01:24.143 --> 00:01:28.780
Image metadata and EXIF concepts may reveal device, time, or location
details.

14
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:33.347
Document metadata may expose authors, comments, paths, or revision
history.

15
00:01:33.347 --> 00:01:39.436
Decompression and resource exhaustion concepts matter when small inputs
expand into large workloads.

16
00:01:39.436 --> 00:01:46.840
Parsing should be isolated, limited, validated, logged appropriately, and
tied to the specific business purpose of the file.
